        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1116,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "For each element, takes the value from one source where a condition holds and from another where it does not. Replace values below a threshold with 0, keep a sensor's reading only where it is confident, fall back to a previous frame where tracking was lost, switch between two signals element by element.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.where <if true> <if false>\nNumbers given here are used until a tensor or number arrives at that inlet: t.where 1 0\nturns a condition into 1s and 0s.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ncondition:\nTrue and false (or non-zero and zero) for each element: usually from t.gt, t.lt, t.isnan... Every condition computes and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nif true:\nThe value used where the condition holds, held: a tensor the condition's shape (or that broadcasts to it), or a single number for every place.\n\nif false:\nThe value used elsewhere, held, likewise.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nselected:\nThe condition's shape, each element from if true or if false.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n-2 5 -1 3 compared with 0 gives the condition. Where it is true the value itself is kept (if true is the values); elsewhere 0 (if false) - every negative value becomes 0.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.where is the numpy version. t.gt and the other comparisons make conditions. t.clamp limits values to a range. t.masked_select keeps only the values where a condition holds.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1100,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "For each element, takes the value from one source where a condition holds and from another where it does not. Replace values below a threshold with 0, keep a sensor's reading only where it is confident, fall back to a previous frame where tracking was lost, switch between two signals element by element.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.where <if true> <if false>\nNumbers given here are used until a tensor or number arrives at that inlet: t.where 1 0\nturns a condition into 1s and 0s.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ncondition:\nTrue and false (or non-zero and zero) for each element: usually from t.gt, t.lt, t.isnan... Every condition computes and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nif true:\nThe value used where the condition holds, held: a tensor the condition's shape (or that broadcasts to it), or a single number for every place.\n\nif false:\nThe value used elsewhere, held, likewise.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nselected:\nThe condition's shape, each element from if true or if false.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n-2 5 -1 3 compared with 0 gives the condition. Where it is true the value itself is kept (if true is the values); elsewhere 0 (if false) - every negative value becomes 0.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.where is the numpy version. t.gt and the other comparisons make conditions. t.clamp limits values to a range. t.masked_select keeps only the values where a condition holds."
        },
